\section{Why this is not a simple comparison}{

Handbook 8.A.38 explains that \code{VISIBLTY} was originally a one-digit code
recording only whether visibility reached the 2-nautical-mile CETAP standard
and, if not, the weather responsible. In 2004 the field was redefined to hold
the actual estimated clear visibility in nautical miles. During the 2021
archive update the old codes were folded back into \code{VISIBLTY} \strong{as negative
numbers}:

\tabular{rl}{
\code{-1} \tab clear visibility for at least 2 nautical miles \cr
\code{-2} \tab less than 2 miles, fog \cr
\code{-3} \tab less than 2 miles, haze \cr
\code{-4} \tab less than 2 miles, rain \cr
\code{-5} \tab less than 2 miles, snow
}

So a plain \code{VISIBLTY >= 2} test — as used by the original processing code —
marks every legacy record as unacceptable, including \code{-1}, which actually
records \emph{good} visibility. On a multi-year dataset that silently discards all
pre-2004 effort.

A \code{-1} record asserts only that visibility reached 2 nmi, so it cannot
satisfy a threshold stricter than 2 and returns \code{NA} in that case rather than
a false \code{TRUE}.
}

